# FEATURE — TIMELINE

CRITICAL — UNIVERSAL SYNCHRONIZATION LAYER

This document describes the Timeline system.
The Timeline is the universal layer used to synchronize actions with song playback.

All changes must preserve timing accuracy, live stability, and deterministic behavior.

⸻

USER GOAL

The user must be able to:
- add actions at precise moments in a song
- trigger MIDI cues automatically
- trigger DMX / lighting cues automatically
- add notes or markers
- adjust timing after capture
- trust that everything follows the music during live playback

⸻

CORE PRINCIPLE

The Timeline is based on song time.

It must NOT depend on:
- lyrics lines
- chord lines
- UI state
- screen refresh rate

👉 One reference only: ExoPlayer currentPosition

⸻

CORE RESPONSIBILITIES

The Timeline is responsible for:

- storing time-based events
- loading events for the current song
- dispatching events during playback
- avoiding duplicate triggers
- handling seek safely
- keeping MIDI / DMX / notes synchronized

👉 Timeline = automation brain

⸻

ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW

Main concepts:

- songId
- timestampMs
- event type
- event payload

Possible event types:
- MIDI
- DMX
- Note
- Marker

Each event belongs to a songId.

⸻

TIMING MODEL

All timeline events must use milliseconds.

Example:

timestampMs = 42500

Means:

42.5 seconds after the start of the song.

Rules:
- no frame-based timing
- no lyric-line timing
- no chord-line timing
- no device-speed timing

👉 Time must always come from playback position.

⸻

PLAYBACK INTEGRATION

During playback:

1. Player reads ExoPlayer currentPosition
2. Timeline evaluates events between previousPosition and currentPosition
3. Matching events are dispatched
4. Dispatchers update external systems or internal state

Important:
- never rely only on exact equality
- always evaluate a time window

Example:

previousPosition < eventTime <= currentPosition

⸻

EVENT DISPATCH SAFETY

Dispatch must be deterministic.

Rules:
- trigger each event once
- avoid duplicate firing
- handle fast position updates
- handle skipped frames
- handle seek forward / backward safely

👉 No random behavior allowed.

⸻

SEEK HANDLING

When user seeks:

- Timeline must resync safely
- Past events must not all retrigger blindly
- Current state may need recomputation depending on event type

Rules:
- MIDI one-shot cues should not spam after seek
- DMX state may need sync to the correct current scene
- Notes / markers should reflect current position

👉 Seek handling must be explicit.

⸻

MIDI CUES

MIDI cues are timeline events.

They may include:
- channel
- message type
- program change value
- control change value
- optional label

Rules:
- MIDI dispatch must be lightweight
- no blocking connection work during playback
- failed MIDI dispatch must not crash playback

👉 Audio must remain priority.

⸻

DMX / LIGHTING CUES

DMX cues are timeline events.

They may include:
- color
- intensity
- strobe
- blackout
- fade duration
- effect duration

Rules:
- DMX computation must be lightweight
- lighting state must be predictable
- no heavy rendering or network blocking on main thread

👉 Lights follow music, never disturb music.

⸻

NOTES / MARKERS

Notes and markers are timeline events used for live reminders.

Examples:
- “Solo”
- “Talk to audience”
- “Change guitar”
- “Prepare next song”

Rules:
- must be readable
- must not block playback
- must not depend on lyrics/chords display

⸻

DATA STORAGE

Timeline data must be stored per songId.

Rules:
- no global anonymous timeline data
- no filename-based linking
- no duplicated storage logic

Timeline data must survive:
- app restart
- song reload
- playlist usage

⸻

EDITING MODEL

User must be able to:
- add an event during playback
- move an event
- edit an event
- delete an event
- test an event when relevant

Editing must:
- update stored data safely
- avoid corrupting the timeline
- preserve unrelated event types

👉 Editing MIDI must not break DMX or notes.

⸻

LIVE CAPTURE

During live capture:

- User taps an action while song plays
- Current ExoPlayer position is captured
- Event is created at that timestamp

Rules:
- capture must be instant
- no heavy dialog blocking live capture
- timestamp must come from ExoPlayer only

⸻

PERFORMANCE RULES

During playback:

FORBIDDEN:
- parsing timeline files repeatedly
- rebuilding the full event list
- sorting large lists every tick
- blocking disk access
- heavy UI recomposition

REQUIRED:
- preload events before playback
- keep runtime structures simple
- dispatch only what is needed

⸻

UI REQUIREMENTS

Timeline UI must allow:
- clear event list
- precise editing
- readable timestamps
- filtering by event type if needed
- safe delete confirmation

During live:
- UI must stay lightweight
- no visual overload
- no animation that risks performance

⸻

KNOWN PITFALLS

- Using lyrics as timeline source
- Triggering cues twice
- Missing cues when polling interval skips over timestamp
- Retiggering all old cues after seek
- Doing disk I/O during playback
- Coupling DMX/MIDI to screen state

👉 These are regression risks.

⸻

TEST SCENARIOS (MANDATORY)

Test at least:

- MIDI cue triggers at correct time
- DMX cue triggers at correct time
- multiple cues close together
- seek forward across cues
- seek backward before cues
- pause / resume
- stop / restart song
- switch song
- edit cue then replay
- delete cue then replay
- playback with lyrics screen visible
- playback with chords screen visible

👉 Must be tested on real device when possible.

⸻

FINAL RULE

The Timeline must ALWAYS be:

- time-based
- deterministic
- independent from UI
- safe during live playback

If a change introduces:
- timing drift
- duplicate triggers
- missed cues
- runtime instability

👉 It must be rejected.